<h2>The inevitable counter-argument</h2>

<p>There is a counter-argument to all of this, and structurally, it is entirely correct: <em>You can lose weight much faster by simply cutting your calories to the floor, skipping the gym entirely, and running an hour a day.</em></p>

<p>If you drop your intake to 1,200 calories and run 50 kilometers a week, the scale will drop rapidly. You do not need to worry about 2g/kg of protein or FAT-MAX zones to make yourself smaller. Starvation and volume work.</p>

<p>The problem is what happens on week six. Your body, recognizing a severe and sustained energy crisis, down-regulates your resting metabolic rate. You lose significant upper body muscle. Your running paces degrade because your glycogen stores are perpetually empty and your fast-twitch fibers have been cannibalized. You feel heavy, your sleep fractures, and the moment you return to a normal eating pattern, the weight rebounds instantly because you now have less muscle mass to burn calories at rest.</p>

<p>Weight loss is a metric. Body composition is a physiology. If you want to drop scale weight for a wedding in three weeks, starve yourself. If you want to build a leaner, more efficient chassis that still knows how to hold a threshold pace, you have to feed the engine and lift the weight.</p>
